In this lesson we'll look at four handy circle theorems that show up again and again in problem solving. First up, the tangent and radius rule. Whenever a line just touches the circle at one point, and you draw the radius out to that same point, the two meet at a perfect right angle. Think of it like a bike wheel resting on the ground, completely flat against it.

Next, the tangent and chord angle, also called the alternate segment theorem. The angle between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact equals the angle sitting in the alternate segment on the other side of the chord. So one little angle on the outside matches another angle tucked away inside the circle.

Then we have equal chords. In the same circle, or in circles of the same size, chords of equal length cut off equal arcs and also subtend equal angles at the centre. Bigger chord, bigger arc, simple as that.

Finally, cyclic quadrilaterals. If all four corners of a quadrilateral sit on one circle, it's called cyclic, and its opposite angles always add up to one hundred and eighty degrees. Chain these together and most angle questions fall open. Now let's see it in action.